Q: Using variable as an instance name in Python A: <p>You should be using <code>self.name</code> . Please understand one thing here that <code>foo='forest' </code>is a string and not a location object. In Python, only the <code>Location</code> instance (like <code>forest</code>) has a <code>.name</code> attribute. A string like <code>&quot;forest&quot;</code> just has string methods (like <code>.upper()</code>), not your custom attributes.</p>
<p>The best way to handle this in simpler structure is by keeping a registry (dict) of <code>Location</code> objects and use a variable that points to the object, not its name</p>
<pre><code>class Location:
    def __init__(self, name, light=1):
        self.name = name
        self.light = light  # 0 = dark, 1 = lit

    def __str__(self):
        return self.name

locations = {
    &quot;forest&quot;: Location(name=&quot;Ye olde forest&quot;, light=0),
    &quot;village&quot;: Location(name=&quot;Quiet village&quot;, light=1),
}
current_location = locations[&quot;forest&quot;]

# Now this works:
if current_location.light == 0:
    print(&quot;It's too dark to see anything here!&quot;)

print(current_location.name)  # &quot;Ye olde forest&quot;
print(str(current_location))  # &quot;Ye olde forest&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Few things that you should be having in mind is accessing attributes on strings like <code>foo.name</code> will never work unless <code>foo</code> is an object. Using <code>globals()</code>/<code>getattr(module, &quot;forest&quot;)</code>,you can technically store <code>forest</code> as a global and then do <code>getattr(sys.modules[__name__], &quot;forest&quot;)</code>, but its hard to maintain as the project grows. Avoid reusing the same variable name for different types (like <code>forest</code> first as a dict, then as a <code>Location</code>). You already converted it, but just be mindful.</p>
